[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:From the Earth: Greetings]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.37]Lesson 55 | 第55课
[00:02.47]From the earth: Greetings | 来自地球的问候
[00:10.35]Which life forms are most likely to develop on a distant planet? | 什么样的生命形式最有可能在遥远的行星上进化？
[00:17.41]Recent developments in astronomy have made it possible to detect planets in our own Milky Way and in other galaxies. | 天文学的最新发展使我们能够在银河系及其他星系中探测到行星。
[00:26.55]This is a major achievement because, in relative terms, planets are very small and do not emit light. | 这是一项重大成就，因为相对而言，行星很小，而且不发光。
[00:34.99]Finding planets is proving hard enough, but finding life on them will prove infinitely more difficult. | 寻找行星已属不易，而要在上面发现生命将无限艰难。
[00:42.52]The first question to answer is whether a planet can actually support life. | 首先要回答的问题是一颗行星是否真的能维持生命。
[00:48.55]In our own solar system, for example, Venus is far to hot and Mars is far too cold to support life. | 例如，在我们自己的太阳系里，金星太热，火星太冷，都无法维持生命。
[00:57.03]Only the Earth provides ideal conditions, and even here it has taken more than four billion years for plant and animal life to evolve. | 只有地球提供了理想的条件，而即使在这里，动植物的进化也花了40多亿年的时间。
[01:08.35]Whether a planet can support life depends on the size and brightness of its star, that is its 'sun'. | 一颗行星是否能维持生命取决于它的恒星——即它的“太阳”——的大小和亮度。
[01:15.68]Imagine a star up to twenty times larger, brighter and hotter than our own sun. | 想象一颗比我们的太阳大20倍、亮20倍、热20倍的恒星。
[01:22.25]A planet would have to be a very long way from it to be capable of supporting life. | 行星必须离它非常远才能维持生命。
[01:27.92]Alternatively, if the star were small, | 或者，如果恒星很小，
[01:31.09]the life-supporting planet would have to have a close orbit round it and also provide the perfect conditions for life forms to develop. | 维持生命的行星就必须紧贴着它运行，并且还要为生命形式的进化提供完美的条件。
[01:41.37]But how would we find such a planet? | 但我们如何找到这样一颗行星呢？
[01:44.73]At present, there is no telescope in existence that is capable of detecting the presence of life. | 目前，还没有一台望远镜能够探测到生命的存在。
[01:52.38]The development of such a telescope will be one of the great astronomical projects of the 21st century. | 研制这样一台望远镜将是21世纪伟大的天文项目之一。
[02:01.15]It is impossible to look for life on another planet using earth-based telescopes. | 使用安置在地球上的望远镜寻找另一颗行星上的生命是不可能的。
[02:07.34]Our own warm atmosphere and the heat generated by the telescope | 我们自己温暖的大气层以及望远镜本身产生的热量
[02:11.98]would make it impossible to detect objects as small as planets. | 使我们无法探测到像行星那样小的物体。
[02:17.10]Even a telescope in orbit round the earth, like the very successful Hubble telescope, | 即使是绕地球轨道运行的望远镜，比如非常成功的哈勃望远镜，
[02:23.32]would not be suitable because of the dust particles in our solar system. | 也不适用，因为我们太阳系中有尘埃颗粒。
[02:29.03]A telescope would have to be as far away as the planet Jupiter to look for life in outer space, | 望远镜必须放到像木星那样遥远的地方去寻找外层空间的生命，
[02:36.55]because the dust becomes thinner the further we travel towards the outer edges of our own solar system. | 因为越往我们太阳系的外缘，尘埃就越稀薄。
[02:43.97]Once we detected a planet, we would have to find a way of blotting out the light from its star, | 一旦我们探测到一颗行星，就必须想办法遮蔽它恒星的光线，
[02:50.47]so that we would be able to 'see' the planet properly and analyse its atmosphere. | 这样才能真正“看见”这颗行星并分析它的大气。
[02:56.38]In the first instance, we would be looking for plant life, rather than 'little green men'. | 首先，我们要寻找的是植物生命，而不是“绿色小人”。
[03:02.99]The life forms most likely to develop on a planet would be bacteria. | 在行星上最有可能进化出的生命形式是细菌。
[03:08.85]It is bacteria that have generated the oxygen we breathe on earth. | 正是细菌在地球上生成了我们呼吸的氧气。
[03:14.08]For most of the earth's history they have been the only form of life on our planet. | 在地球历史的大部分时间里，它们是我们这颗行星上唯一的生命形式。
[03:19.95]As Earth-dwellers, we always cherish the hope that we will be visited by little green men and that we will be able to communicate with them. | 作为地球居民，我们始终怀有这样一个希望：小绿人会来造访我们，而且我们将能与他们交流。
[03:30.24]But this hope is always in the realms of science fiction. | 但这个希望始终只存在于科幻小说的领域。
[03:35.38]If we were able to discover lowly forms of life like bacteria on another planet, it would completely change our view of ourselves. | 如果我们能在另一颗行星上发现像细菌这样的低等生命形式，将彻底改变我们对自己的看法。
[03:45.41]As Daniel Goldin of NASA observed, 'Finding life elsewhere would change everything. | 正如美国国家航空航天局的丹尼尔·戈尔丁所说：“在别处发现生命将改变一切。
[03:52.33]No human endeavour or thought would be unchanged by it.' | 人类的任何努力或思想都不会不因它而改变。”
